.TH FORENSIS-CLI 1 "2026-09" "Forensis 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
forensis \- command line interface for digital forensic investigation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B forensis
[\fIglobal options\fR] \fIcommand\fR
[\fIcommand options\fR] [\fIarguments...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B forensis
is a command line interface for inspecting raw disk images, building a
filesystem-independent forensic model of the objects they contain and
recovering deleted objects. It is aimed at digital forensics analysts,
incident responders and developers who prefer a scriptable, repeatable
workflow over a graphical interface.
.PP
The tool turns the layout of a forensic image into a uniform model.
Bytes are followed from the partition table through the filesystem down
to individual objects, and the same model is shared with the terminal
user interface (forensis-tui), so results can be investigated in the
CLI and the TUI interchangeably.
.PP
Main features:
.IP \[bu] 2
Inspect disk images and enumerate block devices, partitions and filesystems.
.IP \[bu]
Filesystem-independent forensic model of files, directories and deleted objects.
.IP \[bu]
NTFS, ext4 and FAT32 filesystem support in this version.
.IP \[bu]
exFAT filesystem support is planned (roadmap).
.IP \[bu]
Recovery of objects marked as deleted, backed by physical data extraction.
.IP \[bu]
sha256 verification of the recovered bytes: output is checked against the
original object before the recovery is confirmed.
.IP \[bu]
Interactive device source selection when a raw block device is inspected.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B inspect
Inspects a disk image and prints the forensic model: image size, partitions,
filesystems and the forensic objects found in each of them. Accepts the
following options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-detail " \fIid\fR"
Displays detailed information about a single forensic object identified by
its object ID.
.TP
.BR \-\-status " \fIstatus\fR"
Filters forensic objects by status. Accepted values are
\fBnormal\fR, \fBsystem\fR, \fBdeleted\fR, \fBcarved\fR,
\fBinconsistent\fR and \fBunknown\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B tree
Displays the filesystem-independent forensic tree of the image, with a color
legend mapping each status to a color. An optional \fIPATH\fR inside the
filesystem prints only the subtree rooted at that path; if the path does not
exist, it prints
.RB \(oq Path\ not\ found \(cq.
.TP
.B recover deleted
Selects and recovers objects marked as deleted. Lists the recoverable objects,
prompts for a selection (\fB1,3,5\fR for individual objects, \fBA\fR for all,
\fBQ\fR to cancel), and writes the recovered work to the recovery directory
(see RECOVERY WORK DIRECTORY below).
.TP
.B recover all
Runs all currently available recovery methods on the image. At this stage this
includes physical recovery of deleted filesystem objects. Recovered files are
written directly to the directory given by \fB\-\-output\fR.
.TP
.B help, h
Print the list of commands or the help of a specific command.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show help.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version.
.SH FORENSIC STATUS
Objects discovered by
.B forensis
are classified into six statuses, used by
.BR \-\-status ,
by the inspect and tree output and by the terminal UI:
.IP "normal" 4
Active, unmodified object of the filesystem.
.IP "system"
Filesystem metadata object, such as the master file table records.
.IP "deleted"
Object marked as deleted and therefore a candidate for recovery.
.IP "carved"
Object recovered or carved from free space.
.IP "inconsistent"
Object whose forensic metadata is damaged or contradictory.
.IP "unknown"
Object that could not be classified.
.SH RECOVERY WORK DIRECTORY
.B recover deleted
keeps its output in a directory named
.I forensis-recovery
created at the top of the current directory. During the operation the data is
written to
.IR forensis-recovery/.working-<ticket> ,
which is renamed to
.IR forensis-recovery/forensis-recovery-cli-<ticket>
once every selected object has been processed. The ticket is a monotonically
increasing counter stored in
.IR forensis-recovery/.ticket ,
which guarantees every recovery produces a unique directory. If the final
directory already exists, the command fails instead of overwriting previous
work. By contrast,
.B recover all
writes directly to the directory passed with
.BR \-\-output .
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Inspecting a disk image
Inspect a whole image:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis inspect disk.img
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show detailed information about one forensic object by ID:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis inspect disk.img --detail id
.EE
.RE
.PP
List only the objects marked as deleted:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis inspect disk.img --status deleted
.EE
.RE
.SS Displaying the forensic tree
Print the filesystem-independent tree of the image:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis tree disk.img
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print only the subtree of a path inside the filesystem:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis tree disk.img /etc/ssh
.EE
.RE
.SS Recovering deleted objects
Recover deleted objects interactively:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis recover deleted disk.img --output /dest
.EE
.RE
.PP
The command lists the recoverable objects, assigns each one a number and
prompts for the selections:
.PP
.RS
.EX
Recoverable deleted objects:

[1] victim.txt
    Object ID: 64
    Path: /C/evidence/victim.txt

[2] old.db
    Object ID: 71
    Path: /C/evidence/old.db

Select objects to recover [1-5] (e.g. 1,3,5), A=all, Q=cancel: A
.EE
.RE
.PP
After processing, the recovered work is reported with a per-object sha256
verification and saved under the recovery work directory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
Recovery summary
----------------
Selected: 2
Recovered: 2
Failed: 0

Recovery work saved to forensis-recovery/forensis-recovery-cli-00001
.EE
.RE
.SS Recovering with all available methods
Run every recovery method available and write the results to a specific
directory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis recover all disk.img --output /dest
.EE
.RE
.SS Getting help
Print the list of commands or the help of a specific command:
.PP
.RS
.EX
forensis help
forensis help recover
.EE
.RE
.SS Interactive device sources
When a raw block device is inspected, the tool lists the device, its
partitions and any disk images found, then prompts for a numbered choice:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo forensis inspect /dev/sda
.EE
.RE
.SS Running from the source tree
During development the CLI is invoked with
.BR cargo (1)
instead of an installed binary, so the example images of the repository can
be inspected without installing Forensis:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cargo run --bin forensis inspect \e
    lab/ntfs-validation/disk-recursive.img --status deleted
cargo run --bin forensis inspect \e
    lab/ext4-validation/disk-ext4.img --status deleted
cargo run --bin forensis inspect \e
    lab/fat32-validation/disk-fat32.img --status deleted
.EE
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
The
.B recover deleted
subcommand accepts
.BR \-\-output ,
which is used as the destination directory for both the working and the
final recovery locations.
.PP
This version parses the NTFS, ext4 and FAT32 filesystems. Paths that do not
contain a supported filesystem are reported as
.RB \(oq Unsupported\ evidence\ source \(cq.
.PP
Sector geometry is assumed to use 512-byte sectors. Images built with a
different sector size are not detected.
.PP
Recovery operates in memory; processing very large images requires
sufficient RAM to hold the recovered object data.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Sets the logging level of the built-in logger. For example,
\fB\(oqRUST_LOG=debug\(cq\fR enables verbose diagnostic output:
.RS
.EX
RUST_LOG=debug forensis inspect disk.img
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I forensis-recovery/
Recovery work directory, created at the top of the current directory.
.TP
.I forensis-recovery/.ticket
Monotonic counter used to name recovery directories.
.TP
.I <image>
Raw forensic image to inspect (for example
.IR disk.img ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP 0
The command completed successfully.
.IP 1
An error occurred. The error message is printed to standard error preceded by
.RB \(oq Error: \(cq.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR README.md ", " docs/architecture.md " and " docs/forensic-model.md
in the Forensis source tree.
.PP
.BR forensis-tui (1)
.PP
The byte-flow map and design principles:
.I docs/architecture.md
in the Forensis source tree.
